WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.640
Along the Chesapeake Bay, an osprey carries a menhaden home to its chicks.

2
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.240
Menhaden filter tiny plankton out of the water.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.520
Phytoplankton, the plankton that photosynthesize, caught the energy first, from

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.880
sunlight. So where does the osprey's energy come from?

5
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.780
And why does there seem to be less of it at each step of a food chain?

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:28.523
A producer captures energy from sunlight and stores it in food.

7
00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:33.103
In the open water of the Bay, the main producers are phytoplankton.

8
00:00:33.803 --> 00:00:36.863
A consumer gets energy by eating other living things.

9
00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:39.263
Zooplankton eat phytoplankton.

10
00:00:40.223 --> 00:00:41.223
Menhaden eat both.

11
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:43.363
The osprey eats menhaden.

12
00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:45.963
Each feeding step is a trophic level.

13
00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:48.983
Arrows usually point from the food to the eater.

14
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:55.077
When a zooplankton eats a phytoplankton cell, most of that energy does not become

15
00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:59.837
new zooplankton. Some is released as heat during cellular respiration.

16
00:01:00.737 --> 00:01:03.497
Some is in parts that are not eaten or not digested.

17
00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:08.337
Much of that ends up with decomposers, which get energy from dead matter.

18
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:11.157
Only the rest becomes new body tissue.

19
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:17.250
As a rule of thumb, about 10 percent of the energy at one level ends up in the next.

20
00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.850
Here is a model patch of bay, with made-up numbers.

21
00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.210
Phytoplankton add 100,000 kJ per square meter per year.

22
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.780
Zooplankton get 10,000.

23
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.910
Menhaden get 1,000.

24
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.450
The osprey level gets 100.

25
00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.410
Real measurements are often higher or lower.

26
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.150
A 2026 synthesis found an average of about 6

27
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.310
Energy is never made or destroyed.

28
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.010
It changes form. Light becomes chemical energy in food.

29
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.730
Food energy becomes motion and heat.

30
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.590
No living thing can capture that heat to build food again.

31
00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.670
So energy flows one way, in as sunlight and out as heat.

32
00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:01.770
Matter behaves differently.

33
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:08.170
Decomposers release much of the carbon from a dead menhaden as CO2, and

34
00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.750
phytoplankton can take it up again.

35
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:13.033
So where does the osprey's energy come from?

36
00:02:13.793 --> 00:02:16.093
It began as sunlight caught by phytoplankton.

37
00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:21.773
At every step, much of the energy is released as heat or left in uneaten or

38
00:02:21.773 --> 00:02:22.773
undigested matter.

39
00:02:23.653 --> 00:02:27.313
So only a small share, often around 10 passes upward.

40
00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:32.033
Much of the carbon can cycle back to the plankton, but the energy will not.
